<title>GERMANY: Bertelsmann 1995/96 net profit rises.</title>
<headline>Bertelsmann 1995/96 net profit rises.</headline>
<dateline>GUETERSLOH, Germany 1996-09-25</dateline>
<text>
<p>Year to June 30, 1996</p>
<p>(in millions of marks unless stated)</p>
<p>    Group net profit				905  vs    817</p>
<p>    Group operating profit		    1,530  vs  1,665</p>
<p>    Group income from ord. activities     1,309  vs  1,243</p>
<p>    Group net before profit-sharing, tax  1,398  vs  1,349</p>
<p>    Group sales				  21,538  vs 20,554</p>
<p>	 Of which, book publishing	    6,900  up 1 pct</p>
<p>	 Industry division			3,500  up 4 pct</p>
<p>	 BMG Entertainment			8,600  up 9 pct</p>
<p>	 Gruner + Jahr			    4,600  up 4 pct</p>
<p>    Group cash flow			     1,737  vs  1,767</p>
<p>    Group net financial debt (June 30)	260  vs     59</p>
<p>    Group workforce			    57,996  vs 57,397</p>
<p>    Group extraordinary costs		   103  vs    nil</p>
<p>    Depreciation of assets			783  vs    814</p>
<p>    Addition to long-term reserves	     98  vs     60</p>
<p>NOTE - German publishing group Bertelsmann AG said despite the fall in operating profit, net profit rose because of extraordinary income from the sale of its 3.5 percent stake in Luxembourg-based CLT, which was tax-free because it took place outside Germany. Finance chief Siegrfied Luther attributed the fall in operating profit to the weak economy, sluggish private consumption and rising paper costs for its publishing divisions, especially Gruner + Jahr.</p>
<p>-- Bonn newsroom, 49 228 26097150</p>
</text>
